;;; A string written in a rule's `:assert` stops the run — **W18**,
;;; regression `str-in-assert` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, over the names `1` and `a`, and one rule that tags
;;; each with the string `"lbl"`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), written
;;; only after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`: the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at its first string, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one, every command that
;;; saturates stopped at `s`'s first firing, exit 1, because the rule
;;; compiler did not lower a string, and an opaque slot in an `:assert` has
;;; no value to store. `ein saturate FILE` ended with `TypeError: an
;;; unrecognised `:assert` slot has no value to store`, and `ein solve` and
;;; `ein oracle` printed the message alone. Today the program is refused at
;;; the parse: *unexpected `"lbl"`: a string*.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation tag Node Node)
(holds 1) (holds a)
(rule s () :match (holds ?x) :assert (tag ?x "lbl"))
